= 1H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1H}

For a <real number> $\alpha$, set $\alpha_0=\alpha$ and $a_j=\lfloor\alpha_j\rfloor$. If $\alpha_j-a_j\ne0$, define $\alpha_{j+1}=(\alpha_j-a_j)^{-1}$. The resulting <simple continued fraction> is $[a_0;a_1,a_2,\ldots]$, where $a_0$ is an <integer> and subsequent $a_j$ are <positive integers>. It terminates when a <complete quotient> is integral; otherwise its convergents tend to $\alpha$.

For the <continued fraction of the square root of nineteen>, the <complete quotients> after $a_0=4$ are
$$
\frac{\sqrt{19}+4}{3},\quad\frac{\sqrt{19}+2}{5},\quad\frac{\sqrt{19}+3}{2},\quad\frac{\sqrt{19}+3}{5},\quad\frac{\sqrt{19}+2}{3},\quad\sqrt{19}+4.
$$
Their <integer> parts are $2,1,3,1,2,8$. Taking the reciprocal after subtracting $8$ from the last quotient returns the first one, so
$$
\boxed{\sqrt{19}=[4;\overline{2,1,3,1,2,8}].}
$$

= 2F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=2F}

The nonzero <leading coefficients> and distinct degrees make $P_0,\ldots,P_{n-1}$ a <basis> of the <polynomials> of degree at most $n-1$. Consequently the given <orthogonality> implies $\int_a^bf(x)Q(x)dx=0$ for every such <polynomial> $Q$.

If $f$ has infinitely many distinct zeros in $(a,b)$, there is nothing to prove. Otherwise suppose it has fewer than $n$. Among those zeros, list the sign-changing ones as $t_1<\cdots<t_m$, with $m<n$, and put $Q(x)=\prod_{j=1}^m(x-t_j)$, using $Q=1$ when $m=0$. On each interval between zeros, $f$ has a fixed sign by <continuity>. Crossing a sign-changing zero flips both $f$ and $Q$; crossing any other zero flips neither sign. Thus $fQ$ has one constant sign wherever nonzero. Since $f$ is not identically zero, <continuity> gives an <open interval> on which $fQ$ is strictly of that sign. Hence its integral is nonzero, contrary to orthogonality to $Q$. **Therefore $f$ has at least $n$ distinct zeros in $(a,b)$.** This is the reusable <orthogonality forces many interior zeros> argument.

= 3G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry of Group Actions}

= Solution
{parent=3G}

Let $T$ be an <isometry>, and set $S(x)=T(x)-T(0)$. Then $S(0)=0$, and preservation of distances gives $|S(x)|=|x|$. The <polarization identity> yields
$$
\langle S(x),S(y)\rangle=\frac12\bigl(|S(x)|^2+|S(y)|^2-|S(x)-S(y)|^2\bigr)=\langle x,y\rangle.
$$
For a standard <orthonormal basis> $e_1,e_2,e_3$, put $v_i=S(e_i)$. These three vectors are an <orthonormal basis>. Since $\langle S(x),v_i\rangle=x_i$, we have $S(x)=\sum_i x_iv_i=Qx$ for an <orthogonal matrix> $Q$. Thus
$$
\boxed{T(x)=Qx+b,\qquad Q^TQ=I,\quad b=T(0),}
$$
an <affine map>.

For a finite <isometry group> $G$, take $c=|G|^{-1}\sum_{g\in G}g(0)$. An affine map preserves an affine average, so for any $h\in G$,
$$
h(c)=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}h(g(0))=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}g(0)=c.
$$
**The orbit barycentre is a common <fixed point>.** This proves the <fixed point of a finite Euclidean isometry group> result.

= 4G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=4G}

If the possible values have <probabilities> $p_1,\ldots,p_N$, allowing zeros, the <Shannon entropy> in bits is $H(X)=-\sum_i p_i\log_2p_i$, with $0\log_20=0$. Each term is nonnegative, so $H\geq0$, with equality precisely when one value has <probability> one.

For the upper bound, prove first that $\log t\leq t-1$ for $t>0$: the function $t-1-\log t$ has derivative $1-1/t$ and its unique minimum, zero, at $t=1$. Let $m$ be the number of positive <probabilities>. Applying this inequality to $t=(Np_i)^{-1}$ gives
$$
\sum_{p_i>0}p_i\log\frac1{Np_i}\leq\sum_{p_i>0}\left(\frac1N-p_i\right)=\frac mN-1\leq0.
$$
The left side is $H(X)\log2-\log N$, proving
$$
\boxed{0\leq H(X)\leq\log_2N.}
$$
Equality at the maximum requires $m=N$ and equality in every logarithmic inequality, so every $p_i=1/N$. Conversely the <uniform distribution> attains it. For $N=1$ the maximum and minimum coincide at zero.

= 5J
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=5J}

The inverse <logit link> is $\mu_i(\beta)=e^{\beta^Tx_i}/(1+e^{\beta^Tx_i})$. <independence> therefore gives the <Likelihood function> and <log-likelihood>
$$
\boxed{L(\beta;y)=\prod_i\mu_i^{y_i}(1-\mu_i)^{1-y_i},\qquad
\ell(\beta)=\sum_i\left[y_i\beta^Tx_i-\log(1+e^{\beta^Tx_i})\right].}
$$
Differentiation yields the score $\nabla_\beta\ell=\sum_i x_i(y_i-\mu_i)$. The matrix whose rows are $x_i^T$ has dimension $n\times p$, not the PDF's printed $p\times n$. With this necessary dimension correction, any finite maximum <Likelihood function> estimate satisfies
$$
\boxed{X^Ty=X^T\widehat\mu,\qquad
\widehat\mu_i=\frac{e^{\widehat\beta^Tx_i}}{1+e^{\widehat\beta^Tx_i}}.}
$$
The <Hessian matrix> is $-X^TWX$, with $W_{ii}=\mu_i(1-\mu_i)$, so the <log-likelihood> is concave. These score equations apply to a finite interior estimate; complete or quasi-complete separation can instead make its supremum occur only as coefficients diverge.

The <deviance> is twice the difference between maximized saturated and fitted <log-likelihoods>. A saturated <Bernoulli distribution> has fitted means $y_i\in\{0,1\}$ and <log-likelihood> zero, interpreted by limits. Hence the <binomial deviance> here is
$$
\boxed{D(y;\widehat\mu)=2\sum_i\left[y_i\log\frac{y_i}{\widehat\mu_i}+(1-y_i)\log\frac{1-y_i}{1-\widehat\mu_i}\right]
=-2\sum_i\left[y_i\log\widehat\mu_i+(1-y_i)\log(1-\widehat\mu_i)\right].}
$$
Zero-multiplier logarithms are interpreted as zero.

= 6B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=6B}

Continue in the bistable regime $k>2$. At $s=0$ the reaction function factors as $f(g,0)=g(kg-1-g^2)/(1+g^2)$. Besides $g=0$, its positive equilibria are
$$
\boxed{g_1=\frac{k-\sqrt{k^2-4}}2<1,\qquad g_2=\frac{k+\sqrt{k^2-4}}2>1.}
$$
At zero, $f_g=-1$, so it is stable. At a positive equilibrium, use $k=(1+g^2)/g$ to obtain $f_g=(1-g^2)/(1+g^2)$. Therefore $g_1$ is unstable and $g_2$ stable. The sign of $f$ is negative on $(0,g_1)$, positive on $(g_1,g_2)$ and negative beyond $g_2$; these signs determine the requested reaction-rate sketch.

The stationary points of $f(g,0)$ obey $(1+g^2)^2=2kg$. For $k>2$ its smaller solution $g_c<1$ is the local minimum, and its larger solution lies above one and is the local maximum. Since increasing $s$ translates the whole graph upward, the low and middle equilibria collide when
$$
\boxed{s_c=-f(g_c,0)=\frac{g_c(1-g_c^2)}2,\qquad (1+g_c^2)^2=2kg_c.}
$$
For $s>s_c$, both stationary values of $f$ are positive, while $f(g,s)\to-\infty$ as $g\to\infty$; there is exactly one nonnegative equilibrium, on the descending high branch. At equality a double low equilibrium remains, so the uniqueness assertion requires strict exceedance.

For completeness, if the later positive-input request is considered for arbitrary $k>0$, the derivative is $f_g=2kg/(1+g^2)^2-1$. Its maximum is $9k/(8\sqrt3)-1$, attained at $g=1/\sqrt3$. For $k\leq8\sqrt3/9$, the reaction graph is decreasing and every $s>0$ already has a unique steady state, so take $s_c=0$. For $k>8\sqrt3/9$, the smaller positive stationary point gives the same $s_c=g_c(1-g_c^2)/2$; above it the graph crosses zero only on its final descending branch. Persistent robust switching after input is removed uses the previously assumed bistable regime $k>2$.

Starting near zero, raise $s$ past $s_c$ and keep it there long enough for $g$ to pass the unstable threshold and approach the high branch. Decrease $s$ back to zero slowly, or after ensuring $g>g_1$. The trajectory then stays in the basin of the high <stable equilibrium> and ends at the displayed $g_2$. **The persistent switched state is $g_2=(k+\sqrt{k^2-4})/2$.** This is the <saturating autocatalytic switch>; the conclusion presupposes $k>2$ and enough forcing duration, not merely an arbitrarily short pulse of large amplitude.

\Image[paper-1-gene-switch.png]{title=Feedback production and degradation curves showing bistability and a sustained switching pulse}

= 7A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= i
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $k=0$, the <first integral>
$$
E(x,y)=\frac{y^2}{2}+\frac{x^2}{2}-\frac{x^3}{3}
$$
has $\dot E=0$. The <equilibrium points> are $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$. The <linearization> has <eigenvalues> $\pm i$ at the origin and $\pm1$ at $(1,0)$. The origin is a <center equilibrium>: the sufficiently small positive <level sets> of $E$ are closed curves, since its <Hessian matrix> there is <positive-definite>. The other equilibrium is a <saddle equilibrium>.

At the saddle, $E=1/6$, and the <separatrix> satisfies
$$
y^2=\frac{(x-1)^2(2x+1)}3.
$$
Its two branches with $-1/2\leq x\leq1$ form a <homoclinic orbit> through the saddle; its remaining branches extend to $x>1$. Inside the loop, the closed trajectories run clockwise: above the horizontal axis $\dot x=y>0$. The saddle directions have slopes $+1$ and $-1$. Outside the loop there are unbounded trajectories. The left panel shows the closed orbits and all saddle branches.

\Image[paper-1-phase-planes.png]{title=Conservative phase portrait and the attracting basin after adding weak damping}

= ii
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For general $k$, the same <energy> gives $\dot E=-ky^2$. For $k=0.1$ the <eigenvalues> at the origin are $-0.05\pm i\sqrt{3.99}/2$, so it is a <stable focus>. At $(1,0)$ they are $(-0.1\pm\sqrt{4.01})/2$, so it remains a <saddle equilibrium>. The vector field has constant <divergence> $-0.1$: there are no <periodic orbits>, by the planar <divergence> criterion. Nor can the former <homoclinic orbit> survive, since $E$ strictly decreases along every nonconstant orbit except at isolated crossings of $y=0$.

The <stable manifold> of the saddle separates escape to positive $x$ from convergence to the origin. Its two branches approach the saddle in forward time with tangent slope $(-0.1-\sqrt{4.01})/2$. The left unstable branch spirals into the origin, whereas the right unstable branch escapes. The shaded component on the origin's side of the <stable manifold> in the right panel is the <basin of attraction>. The boundary itself is excluded: its trajectories converge to the saddle. This boundary is the damped <stable manifold>, rather than the conservative <energy> contour. <energy> decrease prevents bounded recurrent motion, and every bounded orbit off the saddle's <stable manifold> consequently converges to the origin. The plotted manifold was obtained by integrating backwards from the saddle's stable eigendirection.

= 8C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=8C}

Near zero the magnitude of the integrand is of order $t^{\operatorname{Re}z-1}$, and at infinity it is of order $t^{\operatorname{Re}z-3}$. Thus the <improper integral> converges exactly in the strip
$$
\boxed{0<\operatorname{Re}z<2.}
$$
At a boundary of this strip the logarithmic-variable oscillation has no limiting primitive, so conditional convergence does not enlarge it.

Use a <keyhole contour> about the positive real axis for $w^{z-1}/(1+w)^2$, taking $0<\arg w<2\pi$. The circular arcs vanish in the indicated strip. The upper and lower banks contribute $(1-e^{2\pi iz})F(z)$. The only enclosed <pole> is the <double pole> at $w=-1$, whose <residue> is $(z-1)e^{i\pi z}$. The <residue theorem> therefore gives
$$
(1-e^{2\pi iz})F(z)=2\pi i(z-1)e^{i\pi z},\qquad
\boxed{F(z)=\frac{\pi(1-z)}{\sin\pi z}.}
$$
At $z=1$ the quotient has a <removable singularity>, with limit one, agreeing with $\int_0^\infty(1+t)^{-2}dt=1$.

= 9A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=9A}

For an arbitrary smooth variation $q_i\mapsto q_i+\varepsilon\eta_i$ vanishing at both endpoints,
$$
\delta S=\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\sum_i\left(L_{q_i}\eta_i+L_{\dot q_i}\dot\eta_i\right)dt
=\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\sum_i\left(L_{q_i}-\frac d{dt}L_{\dot q_i}\right)\eta_i\,dt.
$$
The endpoint term in <integration by parts> is zero. <stationarity> for every variation and the <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> imply the <Euler-Lagrange equations> $d(L_{\dot q_i})/dt=L_{q_i}$. Least action here means stationary action, not necessarily a global minimum. The <conjugate momentum> is $p_i=L_{\dot q_i}$; a <cyclic coordinate>, $L_{q_i}=0$, makes $p_i$ constant.

For the <symmetric top>, $\psi$ and $\phi$ are cyclic. Thus
$$
p_\psi=I_3(\dot\psi+\dot\phi\cos\theta)=I_3\omega_3,\qquad
p_\phi=I_1\dot\phi\sin^2\theta+I_3\omega_3\cos\theta
$$
are constant, and consequently so is $\omega_3$. <Conservation of energy from time-translation invariance> also supplies the conserved <energy>
$$
E=\frac12I_1(\dot\theta^2+\dot\phi^2\sin^2\theta)+\frac12I_3\omega_3^2+V(\theta).
$$
These give the requested additional conserved quantities $p_\phi$ and $E$. The $\theta$ equation is
$$
I_1\ddot\theta=I_1\dot\phi^2\sin\theta\cos\theta-I_3\omega_3\dot\phi\sin\theta-V'(\theta).
$$
For constant $\theta$ and constant <precession> rate $\dot\phi$, it reduces to
$$
\boxed{I_1\dot\phi^2\sin\theta\cos\theta-I_3\omega_3\dot\phi\sin\theta=V'(\theta).}
$$
Conversely, choose constants satisfying this balance and set $\dot\psi=\omega_3-\dot\phi\cos\theta$. The cyclic equations and $\theta$ equation are then all satisfied, so this constructs uniform <precession> wherever the Euler-angle chart is regular.

= 10E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= Solution
{parent=10E}

In the nonrelativistic dilute regime, $E(p)=mc^2+p^2/(2m)+\cdots$ and $(mc^2-\mu)/kT\gg1$, so the Bose or Fermi correction in the denominator is negligible. The common <Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution> approximation gives
$$
n\simeq\frac{4\pi g_s}{h^3}e^{(\mu-mc^2)/kT}\int_0^\infty p^2e^{-p^2/(2mkT)}dp.
$$
Differentiating the quoted <Gaussian integral> with respect to its squared scale gives $\int_0^\infty p^2e^{-ap^2}dp=\sqrt\pi/(4a^{3/2})$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{n=g_s\left(\frac{2\pi mkT}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}e^{(\mu-mc^2)/kT}.}
$$
<chemical equilibrium> of $p+n\leftrightarrow D$ requires $\mu_D=\mu_p+\mu_n$. Apply the density formula to each species to obtain
$$
\frac{n_D}{n_nn_p}=\frac{g_D}{g_ng_p}\left(\frac{2\pi kT}{h^2}\right)^{-3/2}
\left(\frac{m_D}{m_nm_p}\right)^{3/2}e^{B_D/kT}.
$$
Using $g_n=g_p=2$, the specified $g_D=4$, and $m_n\simeq m_p$, $m_D\simeq2m_p$, gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{n_D}{n_nn_p}\simeq\left(\frac{\pi m_pkT}{h^2}\right)^{-3/2}e^{B_D/kT}.}
$$
For $X_a=n_a/n_B$ and $n_B=\eta n_\gamma$, the corresponding <deuterium equilibrium abundance> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{X_D}{X_nX_p}\simeq\frac{16\zeta(3)}{\sqrt\pi}\,\eta\left(\frac{kT}{m_pc^2}\right)^{3/2}e^{B_D/kT}.}
$$
The very small <baryon-to-photon ratio> means that even the high-energy tail contains many photons per baryon capable of destroying deuterium when $kT$ is of order $B_D$. Substantial deuterium survives only after that tail has been exponentially suppressed, so $B_D/kT$ must be large enough to offset the small prefactor. This is the <deuterium bottleneck>, not a threshold set just by equality of thermal <energy> and binding <energy>. The degeneracy $g_D=4$ is used as stipulated for this calculation.

= 11G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry of Group Actions}

= Solution
{parent=11G}

<inversion in a circle> with center $c$ and radius $r>0$ is $I(z)=c+r^2/(\overline z-\overline c)$, with $c$ and infinity interchanged. It fixes the circle pointwise and reverses complex orientation. Each inversion is fractional-linear in $\overline z$, so composing two eliminates the conjugation and gives $(az+b)/(cz+d)$ with nonzero <determinant>: a <Möbius transformation>.

Translate and rotate two distinct centers to $0,d$, where $d>0$, and let the radii be $r,s$. The composition of inversion in the first circle followed by the second is
$$
T(z)=d+\frac{s^2z}{r^2-dz}.
$$
Its <fixed points> solve $dz^2-(d^2+r^2-s^2)z+dr^2=0$. The <discriminant> is
$$
\Delta=(d^2-(r+s)^2)(d^2-(r-s)^2).
$$
Disjoint circle boundaries require either $d>r+s$ or $d<|r-s|$, so both factors have the same nonzero sign and $\Delta>0$. There are two distinct <fixed points> on the line of centers. If the circles are concentric, their radii differ and the composition is $z\mapsto(s^2/r^2)z$, with exactly the two <fixed points> $0,\infty$. Thus **the composition has two distinct <fixed points>**, also for nested disjoint circles. Reversing the order inverts the <Möbius transformation> and leaves its <fixed points> unchanged.

= 12G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=12G}

For a <binary symmetric channel> with crossover <probability> $p$, Shannon's noisy coding theorem gives capacity $C=1-h_2(p)$ bits per use, where $h_2(p)=-p\log_2p-(1-p)\log_2(1-p)$. For any rate below capacity there are block codes with block length tending to infinity and decoding error tending to zero. Conversely, vanishing-error transmission cannot have asymptotic rate above capacity. At $p=1/2$ the capacity is zero; the formula also covers $p>1/2$ by complementing the output.

For discrete <random variables>, <mutual information> is
$$
I(X;Y)=\sum_{x,y}p_{xy}\log_2\frac{p_{xy}}{p_xp_y}
=H(X)+H(Y)-H(X,Y).
$$
The formula is symmetric. To prove nonnegativity, apply $\log t\leq t-1$ to $t=p_xp_y/p_{xy}$ for positive joint masses. Multiplication by $p_{xy}$ and summation gives $-I\log2\leq\sum_{p_{xy}>0}p_xp_y-1\leq0$. Hence $I\geq0$, with equality precisely for <independence>. The channel information capacity is $\sup_{p_X}I(X;Y)$, over input distributions.

For the ternary channel every conditional output row has entropy $h_{\rm row}=-(1-2\beta)\log_2(1-2\beta)-2\beta\log_2\beta$, independent of the input. Therefore $I=H(Y)-h_{\rm row}\leq\log_23-h_{\rm row}$. Uniform input produces uniform output, since the matrix is doubly stochastic, and attains the bound. Thus
$$
\boxed{C=\log_23+(1-2\beta)\log_2(1-2\beta)+2\beta\log_2\beta.}
$$
Use $0\log0=0$ at the endpoints. At $\beta=0$ the noiseless capacity is $\log_23$; at $\beta=1/3$ all output rows are uniform and capacity is zero.

= 13J
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=13J}

The array has $K=10$ replicates, $I=2$ levels of factor $a$, and $J=3$ levels of factor $b$, giving $n=60$. R vectorizes an array with the first index changing fastest, then the second, then the third. Accordingly, `gl(I,K,length(y))` repeats each $a$ level for the ten replicates and cycles over its two levels, while `gl(J,K*I,length(y))` repeats each $b$ level for twenty observations. These factors align with the vectorized response.

The first fit is the additive <Analysis of variance> model $Y_{kij}=\mu+\alpha_i+\beta_j+\varepsilon_{kij}$, with independent normal errors of common <variance> $\sigma^2$. Under the default treatment constraints $\alpha_1=\beta_1=0$, the four fitted coefficients estimate the baseline cell mean, the $a=2$ minus $a=1$ effect, and the $b=2,3$ contrasts with $b=1$. Thus the baseline fitted mean is $3.7673$, the second $a$ level adds $3.4542$, and the second and third $b$ levels add $-6.3215$ and $-5.8268$.

The summary $t$ tests compare each indicated coefficient with zero, using its <standard error> and residual <variance> estimate $77.20/56$. They have 56 residual <degrees of freedom>. The three non-intercept contrasts are strongly significant; the intercept test asks whether the baseline mean is zero, not whether either factor matters overall. The <Analysis of variance> tests are joint nested-model $F$ tests: no $a$ main effect, with one numerator degree of freedom, and no $b$ main effect, with two. Their sequential sums of squares are $178.98$ and $494.39$. In this balanced design the main-effect subspaces are orthogonal, so ordering $a,b$ does not change those sums of squares. The denominator is the same residual mean square $77.20/56$; both factor effects are highly significant.

The second fit expands `a*b` to `a+b+a:b`, adding two <interaction> coefficients. It allows the $a$ effect to differ between $b$ levels and has six parameters, equivalently one mean per cell, with 54 residual <degrees of freedom>. The <interaction> adds only about $0.27$ to explained sum of squares; its $F$ statistic is $0.0963$ with <degrees of freedom> $(2,54)$ and $p=0.9084$. There is no evidence against additivity, so the simpler first model is preferable on these data. This does not prove the true <interaction> is exactly zero.

For the final expression, $p=6$, $p_0=4$, and $n=60$. The fitted vectors are orthogonal projections onto nested model spaces, so $\|P Y-P_0Y\|^2$ is the additional sum of squares due to <interaction>. The final code computes
$$
\boxed{F=\frac{\|PY-P_0Y\|^2/2}{\|y-PY\|^2/54}=0.0963\ \text{approximately},}
$$
the same <interaction> test as the <Analysis of variance> row. The exact value uses the original, unrounded observations and fits. Using only the displayed rounded sums $0.27$ and $76.93$ gives about $0.0948$ instead; that discrepancy is rounding, not a different test. The omitted observations prevent reconstructing more digits from the printed data alone.

= 14C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=14C}

The chain rule gives $z_x=\sin2x$, $z_x^2=4z(1-z)$, and $z_{xx}=2(1-2z)$. Therefore
$$
4z(1-z)w_{zz}+2(1-2z)w_z+n^2w=0,
$$
which, after division, is the printed equation. It has only three <regular singular points>, $0,1,\infty$, and is thus a Papperitz equation. At zero and one the indicial roots are $0,1/2$. At infinity, inserting $w\sim z^{-\rho}$ gives $\rho=\pm n/2$. Its <Papperitz symbol> is
$$
\boxed{P\left\{\begin{matrix}0&\infty&1\\0&n/2&0\\1/2&-n/2&1/2\end{matrix};z\right\}.}
$$
The exponent sum is one, as required for a three-singularity second-order equation.

Multiplying the equation by $z(1-z)$ puts it in <Gauss hypergeometric equation> form with $a=n/2$, $b=-n/2$, $c=1/2$. The solution analytic at zero and normalized to one is ${}_2F_1(a,b;c;z)$. Composing it with $z=\sin^2x$ gives an even analytic solution of $w_{xx}+n^2w=0$ with $w(0)=1$, $w_x(0)=0$. Uniqueness of this <ordinary differential equation> implies
$$
\boxed{{}_2F_1(n/2,-n/2;1/2;\sin^2x)=\cos nx.}
$$
This <Gauss hypergeometric function> identity initially holds near zero, and on $|x|<\pi/2$ with the usual real branch; it extends by matching <analytic continuation>. It is not an identity on every real $x$ if the left side is always reevaluated on its principal $z$ branch: for $n=1$ that principal value is $\sqrt{1-\sin^2x}=|\cos x|$. Continuing the branch consistently restores the stated cosine.

= 15E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= i
{parent=15e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let a comoving test particle have physical radius $R=a(t)\chi$, where $\chi$ is fixed. Assume <Newtonian gravity>, spherical homogeneity, negligible pressure, and no mass flux through this sphere, so its enclosed mass $M=(4\pi/3)\rho R^3$ is constant. The <shell theorem> gives a conserved specific <energy>
$$
\frac12\dot R^2-\frac{GM}{R}=\mathcal E.
$$
Substitute $R=a\chi$, divide by $R^2/2$, and write $2\mathcal E/\chi^2=-kc^2$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{H^2=\frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho-\frac{kc^2}{a^2}.}
$$
Homogeneity makes the <energy> scale as $\chi^2$, leaving the same $k$ at every comoving radius. The sign of $k$ encodes the binding-energy sign. This Newtonian derivation applies to pressure-free matter; interpreting $k$ as spatial curvature and extending the equation to relativistic pressure components requires <general relativity>, rather than conservation of this Newtonian shell <energy> alone.

= ii
{parent=15e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Separate fluid conservation gives $\dot\rho_M=-3H\rho_M$, hence $\rho_M=\rho_{M0}a^{-3}$ after normalization at today. For $P_\Lambda=-\rho_\Lambda c^2$, the conservation equation gives $\dot\rho_\Lambda=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\rho=\rho_{M0}a^{-3}+\rho_{\Lambda0}.}
$$
Assume both densities are positive and set $A=8\pi G\rho_{M0}/3$, $B=8\pi G\rho_{\Lambda0}/3$. On the expanding flat branch, $\dot a^2=A/a+Ba^2$. With $b=a^{3/2}$ this becomes $\dot b=(3/2)\sqrt{A+Bb^2}$. Choosing the <big bang> at $t=0$ and integrating gives
$$
\boxed{a(t)=\alpha\sinh^{2/3}(\beta t),\qquad
\alpha=(\rho_{M0}/\rho_{\Lambda0})^{1/3},\quad
\beta=\frac32\sqrt{B}=\sqrt{6\pi G\rho_{\Lambda0}}.}
$$
The normalization $a(t_0)=1$ requires $\sinh(\beta t_0)=\sqrt{\rho_{\Lambda0}/\rho_{M0}}$; equivalently $\alpha=\sinh^{-2/3}(\beta t_0)$. Thus the age $t_0$ and the two positive densities are not independent once this normalization and bang time are fixed.

As $t\downarrow0$, $a\sim\alpha(\beta t)^{2/3}$, the matter-dominated law. As $t\to\infty$, $a\sim\alpha2^{-2/3}e^{\sqrt B\,t}$ and $\ddot a/a\to B>0$, giving accelerated dark-energy expansion. Direct differentiation gives $\ddot a/a=B-A/(2a^3)$, so acceleration begins once $a^3>A/(2B)$. If <dark energy> vanishes, the limiting matter-only solution must be taken instead; negative dark-energy density would not have the stated late-time accelerating form.

= 16G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

= Solution
{parent=16G}

A <well-order> is a linearly ordered set in which every nonempty subset has a least element. First note that a <well-order> cannot be isomorphic to a proper <initial segment> of itself: the least element moved by such an isomorphism would have exactly the same predecessor set on each side, forcing it to be fixed. The same least-disagreement argument makes an isomorphism between <initial segments> unique.

Match $a\in A$ to $b\in B$ exactly when their strict predecessor sets are order-isomorphic. The preceding observation makes each match unique. Matches preserve order and fill <initial segments> on both sides, since restricting an isomorphism matches every earlier element. If both sides had an unmatched element, take the least unmatched $a$ and $b$. Their predecessor sets would be exactly the already matched segments, so they too would match, a contradiction. Therefore the matching exhausts at least one <well-order> and gives an isomorphism of that whole set onto an <initial segment> of the other. Uniqueness follows from the same predecessor-set argument. The whole set is allowed as an <initial segment>; this includes equal <order types>.

For <ordinal> division, let $\beta>0$ and choose
$$
\gamma=\sup\{\eta:\beta\eta\leq\alpha\}.
$$
This supremum is a set <ordinal> because $\beta\eta\geq\eta$ bounds the displayed <ordinals> by $\alpha$. Strict increase and <continuity> of multiplication in the right argument imply $\beta\gamma\leq\alpha<\beta(\gamma+1)$. Take the <order type> $\delta$ of the remaining tail of $\alpha$ after its <initial segment> of type $\beta\gamma$. Then $\alpha=\beta\gamma+\delta$; if $\delta\geq\beta$ it would contradict the strict upper inequality. Thus
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\beta\gamma+\delta,\qquad\delta<\beta.}
$$
The half-open intervals $[\beta\eta,\beta(\eta+1))$ are disjoint, so $\gamma$ is unique, and uniqueness of the tail gives uniqueness of $\delta$. This is left-divisor <ordinal> division: the order of multiplication cannot be interchanged.

Apply it with $\beta=\omega$. The remainder is finite. A nonzero remainder makes $\omega\gamma+\delta$ a successor, while a nonzero $\omega\gamma$ is a limit: for successor $\gamma$ it ends in a full block of type $\omega$, and for limit $\gamma$ it is the supremum of the earlier blocks. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\lambda\ne0\text{ is a limit ordinal}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\lambda=\omega\gamma\text{ for some }\gamma>0.}
$$

= 17F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= Solution
{parent=17F}

<Euler characteristic> for a finite connected plane graph is $v-e+f=2$. For a simple connected <planar graph> with $v\geq3$, each facial boundary has length at least three and the total boundary length is $2e$, so $3f\leq2e$. Combining gives $e\leq3v-6$, and therefore <average degree of a graph> $2e/v<6$. Some vertex has degree at most five. Components of size one or two have this property directly, so every nonempty finite simple <planar graph> has $\delta(G)\leq5$.

Prove <five colour theorem> by induction on vertex count. Remove a vertex $x$ of degree at most five and colour the remaining <planar graph> with five colours. If its neighbours use fewer than five colours, assign an unused colour to $x$. Otherwise its five neighbours $x_1,\ldots,x_5$ occur in cyclic order around $x$ with colours $1,\ldots,5$. If no path of colours $1,3$ connects $x_1$ to $x_3$, interchange these colours in the component of $x_1$, freeing colour 1 at $x$.

If such a path exists, a simple path together with $xx_1$ and $xx_3$ makes a Jordan curve. The cyclic order puts $x_2$ and $x_4$ on opposite sides. A path of colours $2,4$ between them would have to cross the curve, impossible in the plane embedding; it cannot meet the differently coloured path or use the removed vertex. Interchange colours $2,4$ in the component of $x_2$, freeing colour 2 at $x$. The colouring extends in every case. Thus **$\chi(G)\leq5$** for finite simple <planar graphs>. The usual simple-graph convention is needed: loops would invalidate a proper-colouring assertion.

