= Solution
{parent=ii}

**False.** Attach 594 pendant vertices to one vertex of $K_{3,3}$. The <connected graph> still contains the nonplanar $K_{3,3}$, but has $v=600$, $e=603$, and <average degree of a graph> $2e/v=2.01$. Attaching more leaves lowers the average further toward two without making the graph planar.

= iii
{parent=17f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**True** for finite connected simple graphs. <average degree of a graph> at most two implies $e\leq v$. A <spanning tree> has $v-1$ edges, leaving at most one extra edge. Hence the graph is a <tree> or unicyclic. <trees> are planar, and a unicyclic graph is a cycle with <trees> attached to its vertices, which can also be embedded in the plane. Connectedness is essential to this argument: a disconnected nonplanar component can be diluted by isolated vertices.

= 18H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= i
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Over a <finite field> $\mathbb F_q$, the <splitting field> is a finite extension $\mathbb F_{q^m}$, where $m$ is the least common multiple of the degrees of the distinct irreducible factors. Its <Galois group> is cyclic of order $m$, generated by $x\mapsto x^q$.

Over $\mathbb F_2$, the <polynomial> has no linear factor: its values at zero and one are both one. The only irreducible quadratic is $x^2+x+1$; reduction modulo it gives $x$, not zero. A reducible quartic without linear factors would split into quadratics, so $f$ is irreducible. Thus its <splitting field> is $\mathbb F_{16}$ and
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}(f/\mathbb F_2)\simeq C_4.}
$$

= ii
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Direct division gives $f=(x-1)(x^3+2x^2+2x+2)$ over $\mathbb F_3$. The cubic takes values $2,1,1$ at $0,1,2$, so is irreducible. Finite-field extensions are cyclic <Galois extensions>, and the <splitting field> degree is the least common multiple of these factor degrees, namely three. Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}(f/\mathbb F_3)\simeq C_3,\qquad\text{splitting field }\mathbb F_{27}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <splitting field> over $\mathbb F_2$ is $\mathbb F_{16}$, and it contains the unique subfield $\mathbb F_4$. Over $\mathbb F_4$, Frobenius is the square of the order-four $\mathbb F_2$ Frobenius, so it has two orbits of length two on the four roots. Thus $f$ factors into two irreducible quadratics, and
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}(f/\mathbb F_4)\simeq C_2,}
$$
generated by $x\mapsto x^4$ on $\mathbb F_{16}$.

= iv
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Irreducibility modulo two implies irreducibility over $\mathbb Q$ by <Gauss lemma for polynomials>, so the <Galois group> embeds transitively in $S_4$. The <discriminant> is $229$, hence primes two and three are unramified. The factor-degree theorem says that square-free reduction with factor degrees $d_1,\ldots,d_r$ provides an element of the <Galois group> with cycle lengths $d_1,\ldots,d_r$. The reductions already found therefore give a 4-cycle and a 3-cycle.

The <group> order divides 24 and is divisible by four and three, so is 12 or 24. An order-12 subgroup of $S_4$ has index two and must be $A_4$: in its quotient every <transposition> has the same nontrivial image, since <transpositions> are conjugate and generate $S_4$, making the quotient map the sign. But a 4-cycle is odd. Therefore the order is 24 and
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}(f/\mathbb Q)=S_4.}
$$
The <discriminant> can also be obtained from $f'=x^2(4x+3)$ and the <resultant>, giving $256-27=229$.

= 19G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= Solution
{parent=19G}

For $\ell\in V^*$ define $(g\ell)(v)=\ell(g^{-1}v)$. The representing matrix is the transpose of the inverse matrix on $V$, so the dual <character> is
$$
\boxed{\beta(g)=\alpha(g^{-1})=\overline{\alpha(g)}.}
$$
The last equality follows by averaging a <Hermitian inner product> to make the finite-group <group representation> unitary. Complex finite-group representations are isomorphic exactly when their <characters> agree. Hence $V$ is self-dual exactly when its <character> is real-valued.

The invariant tensor identification $(V\otimes V)^G\simeq\operatorname{Hom}_G(V^*,V)$ and <Schur lemma> show that, for irreducible $V$, the trivial multiplicity is zero unless $V^*\simeq V$, and one in that case. Dually, a self-duality isomorphism gives a nonzero invariant <bilinear form> $B$ on $V$. Its radical is an invariant subspace, so irreducibility makes $B$ nondegenerate. The space of invariant <bilinear forms> is one dimensional. Since the transpose $B^T$ is another such form, $B^T=cB$; transposing again gives $c^2=1$. Therefore $B$ is symmetric or alternating: in the negative-transpose case, $B(v,v)=-B(v,v)$ forces $B(v,v)=0$. Both types cannot coexist, since proportional nonzero forms cannot have both transpose signs.

In odd dimension an alternating matrix has <determinant> zero: $\det B=\det(-B^T)=(-1)^n\det B=-\det B$. Thus only the symmetric case is possible. Over $\mathbb C$ a nondegenerate <symmetric bilinear form> has a <basis> with matrix $I$: diagonalize it by congruence and rescale each nonzero diagonal entry. In that <basis> invariance says $\rho(g)^T\rho(g)=I$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\rho(G)\text{ is conjugate to a subgroup of }O(n,\mathbb C)\quad(n\text{ odd, irreducible self-dual}).}
$$

= 20G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Number Fields}

= a
{parent=20g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <ideal class group> of a <number field> is the <group> of nonzero <fractional ideals> of its <ring of integers> under multiplication, modulo the subgroup of principal <fractional ideals>. For degree $n$ and $r_2$ pairs of complex embeddings, the <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> states that each class contains an <integral ideal> $I$ with
$$
N(I)\leq\left(\frac4\pi\right)^{r_2}\frac{n!}{n^n}\sqrt{|D_K|}.
$$
There are only finitely many <integral ideals> of bounded <ideal norm>: as sublattices of the free abelian <group> of <integers>, they have bounded index, and finitely many subgroups have any fixed index. Thus the <ideal class group> is finite.

= b
{parent=20g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The element $\omega$ satisfies $\omega^2-\omega+6=0$, so is an <algebraic integer>. Conversely, write an <algebraic integer> as $(a+b\sqrt{-23})/2$ with $a,b$ rational. Its trace gives $a\in\mathbb Z$, and its <field norm> implies $23b^2\in\mathbb Z$. If $b=p/q$ in lowest terms, $q^2$ divides 23, forcing $q=1$. The integral <field norm> condition modulo four then makes $a,b$ have the same parity. Therefore it equals $(a-b)/2+b\omega$, proving
$$
\boxed{\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z+\mathbb Z\omega,\qquad D_K=-23.}
$$
Both reductions of $x^2-x+6$ modulo two and three split into the distinct roots zero and one. The index is one, so ideal factorization gives
$$
(2)=\mathfrak p_2\overline{\mathfrak p}_2,\quad\mathfrak p_2=(2,\omega),\quad\overline{\mathfrak p}_2=(2,\omega-1),
$$
$$
(3)=\mathfrak p_3\overline{\mathfrak p}_3,\quad\mathfrak p_3=(3,\omega),\quad\overline{\mathfrak p}_3=(3,\omega-1).
$$
Their <norms> are two and three respectively. The product $\mathfrak p_2\mathfrak p_3$ is generated by $6,2\omega,3\omega,\omega^2$. All are multiples of $\omega$, using $6=\omega\overline\omega$; and $\omega=3\omega-2\omega$ belongs to the product. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak p_2\mathfrak p_3=(\omega).}
$$
The Minkowski bound is $(2/\pi)\sqrt{23}<4$, in fact about $3.05$. Every class therefore has a representative of <ideal norm> one, two or three. The displayed primes and their conjugates exhaust those possibilities. Their classes are all $1,c,c^{-1}$, with $c=[\mathfrak p_2]$, because $[\mathfrak p_3]=c^{-1}$ and conjugation inverts a quadratic ideal class. There are at most three classes.

There is no element of <field norm> two: $N(a+b\omega)=a^2+ab+6b^2=(a+b/2)^2+23b^2/4$, which exceeds two for $b\ne0$ and is a square for $b=0$. Thus $\mathfrak p_2$ is not principal. Moreover $N(\omega-2)=8$, and $\omega-2$ belongs to $\mathfrak p_2$ but not its conjugate, so $(\omega-2)=\mathfrak p_2^3$. Consequently $c$ has order three, and
$$
\boxed{h_K=3.}
$$

= 21F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= i
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <van Kampen theorem> says: if $X=U\cup V$ with $U,V$ open and path connected, $U\cap V$ path connected, and a base point in the intersection, then $\pi_1(X)$ is the <free product> $\pi_1(U)*\pi_1(V)$ modulo the <normal subgroup> generated by identifying the two images of every element of $\pi_1(U\cap V)$. Equivalently it is the <pushout> of the two inclusion-induced fundamental-group maps.

= ii
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose open neighborhoods of the sphere and circle in the wedge, each enlarged by a small contractible neighborhood of the attaching point in the other piece. They <deformation retraction> to $S^2$ and $S^1$ and have contractible intersection. Van Kampen therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\pi_1(S^2\vee S^1)=\pi_1(S^2)*\pi_1(S^1)=\mathbb Z.}
$$
The generator traverses the circle once; the sphere contributes no fundamental-group generator.

= iii
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A geometric circle is an <unknot>. Compactify $\mathbb R^3$ to $S^3$, and carry the circle to $\{(z_1,z_2)\in\mathbb C^2:|z_1|^2+|z_2|^2=1,\ z_2=0\}$. Its complement has coordinates $z_1\in\Delta$ and $z_2=\sqrt{1-|z_1|^2}e^{i\theta}$, making it homeomorphic to $\Delta\times S^1$. It deformation retracts to $S^1$. Removing the additional point at infinity does not change the <fundamental group>: van Kampen for a small ball about that point uses a <simply connected> punctured ball, homotopy equivalent to $S^2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\pi_1(\mathbb R^3\setminus A)=\mathbb Z.}
$$
A meridian linking the circle once generates it. This uses “circle” in its geometric sense; the complement of an arbitrary knotted embedded copy of $S^1$ need not have this <group>.

= 22F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=22F}

Concavity of $\log$ gives $\log(a/p+b/q)\geq p^{-1}\log a+q^{-1}\log b$ because $1/p+1/q=1$. Exponentiating proves <Young inequality>
$$
\boxed{a^{1/p}b^{1/q}\leq a/p+b/q,}
$$
with equality precisely when $a=b$. Equivalently it follows from convexity of the exponential.

Define $\ell^p=\{x=(x_j):\sum_j|x_j|^p<\infty\}$ with $\|x\|_p=(\sum_j|x_j|^p)^{1/p}$. Positivity, definiteness and homogeneity are immediate. Summing <Young inequality> for normalized sequences gives <Hölder's inequality> $\sum|x_jy_j|\leq\|x\|_p\|y\|_q$. For a finite truncation $z=x+y$, it yields
$$
\sum|z_j|^p\leq\sum(|x_j|+|y_j|)|z_j|^{p-1}
\leq(\|x\|_p+\|y\|_p)\left(\sum|z_j|^p\right)^{1/q}.
$$
Dividing and passing to larger truncations proves <Minkowski inequality> and shows $x+y\in\ell^p$, so this is a <normed vector space>.

Let $x^{(m)}$ be a norm-Cauchy sequence. Each coordinate is Cauchy because $|x_j^{(m)}-x_j^{(l)}|\leq\|x^{(m)}-x^{(l)}\|_p$, and let its limit be $x_j$. The sequence <norms> are bounded by some $C$. For every finite $N$, passage to coordinate limits gives $\sum_{j\leq N}|x_j|^p\leq C^p$, so $x\in\ell^p$. Given $\varepsilon>0$, choose $M$ with $\|x^{(m)}-x^{(l)}\|_p<\varepsilon$ for $m,l\geq M$. Letting $l\to\infty$ in finite truncations and then $N\to\infty$ gives $\|x^{(m)}-x\|_p\leq\varepsilon$ for $m\geq M$. **Thus $\ell^p$ is complete, hence a <Banach space>.**

= 23H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= Solution
{parent=23H}

A <Riemann surface> is a connected one-complex-dimensional manifold with <holomorphic> chart transitions. A map between such surfaces is <holomorphic> when all its local coordinate expressions are <holomorphic>; it is <biholomorphic> when it is a bijection whose inverse is also <holomorphic>.

The open mapping theorem says a nonconstant <holomorphic> function on a connected complex domain maps open sets to open sets. Applied in charts, with the <identity theorem> excluding a locally constant portion, it makes a nonconstant <holomorphic map> of connected surfaces open. If the domain is compact, its image is compact and hence closed in the Hausdorff target. A nonempty set both open and closed in a connected target is the whole target. Therefore the map is surjective, without any appeal to degree.

For a biholomorphism $\phi:\Delta^*\to\Delta^*$, both $\phi$ and its inverse are bounded near the missing origin. They extend holomorphically across zero by removability: in a <Laurent series> a negative coefficient satisfies $|a_{-m}|\leq M r^m$ on radius $r$, hence vanishes as $r\to0$. Let the extensions be $F,G$. Their images lie in $\Delta$: a modulus-one interior value would force a bounded <holomorphic map> to be constant by the <maximum modulus principle>. The compositions $GF$ and $FG$ equal the identity on the punctured disk and thus on the disk by <continuity>. If $F(0)\ne0$, then $G(F(0))$ would be nonzero because the original inverse maps $\Delta^*$ into itself, contradicting $G(F(0))=0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{F:\Delta\to\Delta\text{ is biholomorphic and }F(0)=0.}
$$

For the final assertion only <continuity> on $R$ and holomorphicity on $R\setminus\{p\}$ are needed; the printed stronger description is redundant. Choose a target chart about $f(p)$ and, by <continuity>, a small source chart mapped into it. The resulting scalar function is <holomorphic> on a punctured disk and bounded near its center, so the same Laurent argument removes its singularity. <continuity> makes the extension's central value equal the prescribed value. Thus $f$ is <holomorphic> also at $p$.

= 24H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= a
{parent=24h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <determinant> is continuous and $GL(n)=\det^{-1}(\mathbb R\setminus\{0\})$ is open in $M(n)\simeq\mathbb R^{n^2}$. Every open subset inherits the ambient smooth charts, so it is a <submanifold> and
$$
\boxed{\dim GL(n)=n^2.}
$$

= b
{parent=24h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For an invertible $A$, factor $\det(A+tB)=\det A\det(I+tA^{-1}B)$. The <determinant>'s permutation expansion gives $\det(I+tC)=1+t\operatorname{tr}C+O(t^2)$. Differentiating at zero yields
$$
\boxed{(d\det)_A(B)=\det A\,\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1}B).}
$$
At a determinant-one matrix choose $B=A/n$; the derivative is one. Thus one is a <regular value> of the smooth <determinant> map on $GL(n)$. The <regular level set theorem> makes $SL(n)$ a <submanifold> of <codimension of a submanifold> one, with
$$
\boxed{\dim SL(n)=n^2-1.}
$$
This includes $n=1$, when $SL(1)=\{1\}$ has dimension zero.

= c
{parent=24h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $n=1$, the singular set is the point $\{0\}$, a zero-dimensional <submanifold>. For $n\geq2$, every elementary matrix $E_{ij}$ is singular and the smooth curve $t\mapsto tE_{ij}$ lies in $X$ through zero. If $X$ were a <submanifold>, all $E_{ij}$ would belong to $T_0X$ and span $M(n)$. Its dimension at zero would therefore be $n^2$, making $X$ locally open there. But arbitrarily small matrices $tI$ are invertible, a contradiction. Thus
$$
\boxed{X\text{ is a submanifold exactly when }n=1.}
$$
The failure is at the zero matrix; fixed-rank pieces of the singular set can separately be smooth <submanifolds>.

= 25J
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= Solution
{parent=25J}

The <dominated convergence theorem> states that if measurable functions converge almost everywhere and their absolute values are bounded by a single <Lebesgue integrable function>, their limit is integrable and their integrals converge to its integral.

Put $b_j=a_j/j$. The hypothesis makes $b_j$ summable. On the <positive integers> with <counting measure> define $f_n(j)=(j/n)b_j\mathbf1_{j\leq n}$. For each fixed $j$ it tends to zero and $0\leq f_n(j)\leq b_j$. Dominated convergence gives
$$
\boxed{\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^n a_j=\sum_jf_n(j)\longrightarrow0,}
$$
the stated <Kronecker lemma>.

For a bounded interval union $B$, $S_j\sim N(0,j)$ has density bounded by $1/\sqrt{2\pi j}$. Hence $\mathbb P(S_j\in B)\leq |B|/\sqrt{2\pi j}$, and
$$
\mathbb E\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac{\mathbf1_B(S_j)}j
\leq\frac{|B|}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\sum_{j=1}^\infty j^{-3/2}<\infty.
$$
By <Tonelli's theorem>, the nonnegative series is finite <almost surely>. <Kronecker lemma> then makes the empirical occupation fraction of $B$ tend to zero. The family of finite rational-endpoint interval unions is countable, so intersecting their probability-one events proves simultaneous convergence for all of them. For the whole line, every indicator is one. Thus
$$
\boxed{m(B)=0\text{ for bounded }B\in F_0,\qquad m(\mathbb R)=1,\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$
There is no countably additive <Borel measure> extending these values: $\mathbb R=\bigcup_{n\geq1}(-n,n)$ would have measure zero by <continuity> from below, contrary to its assigned measure one. The escaped empirical mass cannot be captured by a <probability> measure on the real line.

This is the <Gaussian random walk spends zero density of time in bounded sets> phenomenon.

= 26I
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= i
{parent=26i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <continuous-time Markov chain> started with distribution $\pi$ is <reversible> if its finite-dimensional distributions are unchanged by reversing time: for every $T>0$, $(X_t:0\leq t\leq T)$ and $(X_{T-t}:0\leq t\leq T)$ have the same law. In particular, reversibility includes <stationarity>. The <detailed balance> equations are
$$
\boxed{\pi_iq_{ij}=\pi_jq_{ji}\quad(i,j\in I).}
$$
For a finite <continuous-time Markov chain>, these equations are equivalent to reversibility with initial distribution $\pi$. Equivalently, they imply $\pi_iP_{ij}(t)=\pi_jP_{ji}(t)$ for every transition matrix $P(t)=e^{tQ}$.

= ii
{parent=26i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $\pi$ be a <probability> vector satisfying <detailed balance>. Since each row of the <infinitesimal generator matrix> sums to zero,
$$
(\pi Q)_j=\sum_i\pi_iq_{ij}=\pi_j\sum_iq_{ji}=0.
$$
Thus $\pi Q=0$, and $\pi e^{tQ}=\pi$ for all $t\geq0$. This proves that $\pi$ is an <stationary distribution>. The diagonal term causes no problem because its <detailed balance> equation is an identity.

= iii
{parent=26i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A <discrete-time Markov chain> with initial distribution $\widehat\pi$ is <reversible> if $(Y_0,\ldots,Y_n)$ and $(Y_n,\ldots,Y_0)$ have the same law for every $n$. Its <detailed balance> equations are $\widehat\pi_i\widehat P_{ij}=\widehat\pi_j\widehat P_{ji}$. These equations are equivalent to reversibility. Indeed, reversing every factor in the <probability> $\widehat\pi_{i_0}\prod_{r=1}^n\widehat P_{i_{r-1}i_r}$ gives the reverse path <probability>; the converse follows by taking $n=1$.

Summing <detailed balance> over $i$ gives
$$
(\widehat\pi\widehat P)_j=\sum_i\widehat\pi_i\widehat P_{ij}=\widehat\pi_j\sum_i\widehat P_{ji}=\widehat\pi_j.
$$
Hence $\widehat\pi$ is an <stationary distribution>.

= iv
{parent=26i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <jump chain> records the successive states visited at actual jumps of the <continuous-time Markov chain>. Put $q_i=-q_{ii}$. For an irreducible chain with at least two states, all $q_i>0$, and its transition <probabilities> are $\widehat P_{ij}=q_{ij}/q_i$ for $j\ne i$, with $\widehat P_{ii}=0$. The holding time in state $i$ is exponential with mean $1/q_i$.

If $\pi Q=0$, set $C=\sum_i\pi_iq_i$ and $\widehat\pi_i=\pi_iq_i/C$. Then
$$
(\widehat\pi\widehat P)_j=\frac1C\sum_{i\ne j}\pi_iq_{ij}=\frac{\pi_jq_j}{C}=\widehat\pi_j.
$$
Conversely, $\widehat\pi\widehat P=\widehat\pi$ implies that $\pi_i=c\widehat\pi_i/q_i$, normalized to sum one, satisfies $\pi Q=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\widehat\pi_i=\frac{\pi_iq_i}{\sum_j\pi_jq_j},\qquad \pi_i=\frac{\widehat\pi_i/q_i}{\sum_j\widehat\pi_j/q_j}.}
$$
Jump observations overrepresent states with short holding times. A one-state chain has no actual jumps; its trivial equilibrium can be handled separately, without using this division by $q_i=0$.

= v
{parent=26i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Use the equilibrium correspondence from the preceding part. For $i\ne j$,
$$
\widehat\pi_i\widehat P_{ij}=\frac{\pi_iq_{ij}}C.
$$
Consequently the <detailed balance> equations for the <jump chain> hold exactly when those for the <continuous-time Markov chain> hold. The reversibility characterizations already given prove the equivalence. Both chains here are started in their respective <stationary distributions>, which generally differ.

= vi
{parent=26i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

Write $q_{ij}=c m_{ij}$ for $i\ne j$, with a common positive constant $c$, and put $d_i=\sum_{j\ne i}m_{ij}$. The <infinitesimal generator matrix> is symmetric because the graph is undirected. Thus the <uniform distribution> $\pi_i=1/|I|$ satisfies <detailed balance>, and the <continuous-time random walk> is reversible when started uniformly.

Its <jump chain> chooses a link uniformly from those incident to its current vertex:
$$
\widehat P_{ij}=\frac{m_{ij}}{d_i},\qquad \boxed{\widehat\pi_i=\frac{d_i}{\sum_jd_j}.}
$$
The flux $\widehat\pi_i\widehat P_{ij}=m_{ij}/\sum_jd_j$ is symmetric, so this <random walk on a multigraph> is also reversible. The continuous-time equilibrium is uniform because higher-degree vertices have shorter holding times; at jump times the equilibrium is degree-weighted. If the intended proportionality constant depends on the starting vertex, $q_{ij}=c_i m_{ij}$, reversibility still holds with $\pi_i\propto1/c_i$, and the same jump chain results.

= 27I
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= Solution
{parent=27I}

For the fixed observation interval, $N_3$ has <Poisson distribution> with mean and <variance> $3\lambda$. Hence $N_3/3$ is unbiased and has <variance> $\lambda/3$. Its <score function> is $N_3/\lambda-3$, and its <Fisher information> is $3/\lambda$. The <Cramer-Rao bound> for unbiased estimation of $\lambda$ therefore gives <variance> at least $\lambda/3$, attained by $N_3/3$.

For the stopping-time observation, $T_{10}$ has <Gamma distribution> with shape $10$ and rate $\lambda$. Direct integration of its density gives
$$
\mathbb E_\lambda(T_{10}^{-1})=\frac\lambda9,\qquad
\mathbb E_\lambda(T_{10}^{-2})=\frac{\lambda^2}{9\cdot8}.
$$
Thus $k=9$, and
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\Lambda_2=9/T_{10},\qquad \operatorname{Var}(\widetilde\Lambda_2)=\lambda^2/8.}
$$
The <score function> is $10/\lambda-T_{10}$, so the <Fisher information> is $10/\lambda^2$. Its <Cramér-Rao lower bound> is $\lambda^2/10$, strictly below this estimator's <variance>.

Nevertheless, $9/T_{10}$ is <uniformly minimum-variance unbiased>. The observed $T_{10}$ is a <sufficient statistic>, and its family is complete: if $\mathbb E_\lambda h(T_{10})=0$ for all $\lambda>0$, then $\int_0^\infty h(t)t^9e^{-\lambda t}\,dt=0$ for all $\lambda>0$. Uniqueness of the <Laplace transform>, applied after an exponential tilt to make an <Lebesgue integrable function>, implies $h=0$ almost everywhere. The <Lehmann–Scheffé theorem> now proves the minimum-variance assertion. Failure to attain the Cramér–Rao bound does not disprove minimum <variance> within the class of <unbiased estimators>.

For the actual observations, the two <likelihood functions> are
$$
L_1(\lambda)=\frac{(3\lambda)^{10}}{10!}e^{-3\lambda},\qquad
L_2(\lambda)=\frac{\lambda^{10}3^9}{9!}e^{-3\lambda}.
$$
They differ by a factor independent of $\lambda$. The <likelihood principle> consequently requires the same inferential conclusions, since the observed data carry proportional likelihoods. But the two minimum-variance unbiased estimates are $\boxed{10/3\text{ and }3}$ respectively. Minimum-variance unbiased estimation therefore violates the <likelihood principle>: its unbiasedness and <variance> refer to the sampling plan, including the stopping rule, rather than solely to the observed <Likelihood function>.

= 28J
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= a
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For positive $S_0,K,T,\sigma$, the <Black–Scholes formula> uses
$$
\boxed{d_1=\frac{\log(S_0/K)+(r+\sigma^2/2)T}{\sigma\sqrt T},\qquad d_2=d_1-\sigma\sqrt T.}
$$
To hedge the sold <European call option>, buy $\Phi(d_1)$ shares and borrow $K e^{-rT}\Phi(d_2)$ in cash. This long <replicating strategy> has initial value $S_0\Phi(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}\Phi(d_2)$, financed by the option premium. The share holding must subsequently be rebalanced according to the option's <option delta> in a <self-financing strategy>.

= b
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

As $\sigma\to\infty$, $d_1\to+\infty$ and $d_2\to-\infty$, so the <European call option> price tends to $\boxed{S_0}$. If its traded price $C>S_0$, sell a call, buy one share, and invest the surplus $C-S_0$ at the risk-free rate. The initial net cost is zero, and the maturity payoff is
$$
S_T-(S_T-K)^++(C-S_0)e^{rT}=\min(S_T,K)+(C-S_0)e^{rT}>0.
$$
This is an explicit <arbitrage>.

= c
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

As $\sigma\downarrow0$, the <Black–Scholes formula> tends to $\boxed{(S_0-Ke^{-rT})^+}$. This also follows by discounting the deterministic zero-volatility payoff; at equality $S_0=Ke^{-rT}$ the limiting value is zero.

If $S_0>Ke^{-rT}$ and a call trades at $C<S_0-Ke^{-rT}$, buy a call, short one share, and buy a bond paying $K$ at maturity. Invest the positive initial surplus $S_0-Ke^{-rT}-C$. At maturity the call, share and bond together pay $(S_T-K)^+-S_T+K=(K-S_T)^+\geq0$, in addition to the strictly positive invested surplus. If the lower bound is zero, violating it means $C<0$: buy the call and invest the cash received, obtaining a nonnegative option payoff and a strictly positive bank payoff. Both strategies are <arbitrages>.

= d
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write $\phi=\Phi'$ for the standard <normal density>. Differentiating the <Black–Scholes formula> and using $S_0\phi(d_1)=Ke^{-rT}\phi(d_2)$ gives
$$
\frac{\partial C}{\partial\sigma}
=S_0\phi(d_1)\left(\frac{\partial d_1}{\partial\sigma}-\frac{\partial d_2}{\partial\sigma}\right)
=\boxed{S_0\sqrt T\,\phi(d_1)>0}.
$$
The last equality follows by differentiating $d_1-d_2=\sigma\sqrt T$. This derivative is the option's <vega>.

= e
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <Black-Scholes implied volatility> of a traded call price $C$ is the volatility $\sigma$ which makes the <Black–Scholes formula> equal to $C$. For
$$
(S_0-Ke^{-rT})^+<C<S_0,
$$
the price is a continuous strictly increasing function of $\sigma>0$, by its positive <vega>, with precisely these endpoint limits. The <intermediate value theorem> and strict monotonicity therefore give a unique positive implied volatility. The lower endpoint corresponds to the limiting value $\sigma=0$; the upper endpoint is reached only as $\sigma\to\infty$. Prices outside the bounds admit the <arbitrages> above and have no implied volatility.

= 29C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Partial Differential Equations}

= i
{parent=29c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a first-order <quasilinear partial differential equation>, take $C^1$ coefficients $a_j,b$, $C^1$ prescribed data $u_0$ on a smooth $C^2$ <hypersurface> $S$, and a point $x_0\in S$. The data are non-characteristic there if
$$
a(x_0,u_0(x_0))\cdot n(x_0)\ne0,
$$
where $n$ is any nonzero normal to $S$. Then there is a neighborhood of $x_0$ on which a unique $C^1$ solution realizes the data. Uniqueness is local, after restricting to a neighborhood swept out by the nearby characteristics.

The <method of characteristics> solves $\dot x=a(x,u)$, $\dot u=b(x,u)$ from points of $S$. In local surface coordinates $\eta$, the map $(\eta,s)\mapsto x(\eta,s)$ has nonsingular derivative at $s=0$ exactly under the stated normal condition. The <inverse function theorem> makes these variables local coordinates, giving the asserted solution and uniqueness.

= ii
{parent=29c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here the <characteristic curves> are straight lines $x=y+sa$, $y\cdot n=0$, along which $\dot u=c\cdot(y+sa)+d$. If $a\cdot n\ne0$, every nearby point determines a unique starting point and travel parameter:
$$
s=\frac{x\cdot n}{a\cdot n},\qquad y=x-sa.
$$
Integrating along the <Characteristic curve> gives
$$
\boxed{u(x)=u_0(y)+s(c\cdot y+d)+\frac{s^2}{2}\,c\cdot a.}
$$
The non-characteristic condition is exactly what permits division by $a\cdot n$ and ensures transversality to the data hyperplane. If $a\cdot n=0$, the <Characteristic curve> lines starting there never leave it. Moreover the prescribed data must satisfy $a\cdot\nabla_Su_0=c\cdot x+d$ on the hyperplane even to be compatible. If they do, the <Characteristic curve> construction still leaves off-hyperplane data undetermined; if they do not, no $C^1$ solution exists.

= iii
{parent=29c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Start a <characteristic curve> at $(0,\eta)$ with $u=-\eta$. The <Characteristic curve> equations give $u=-\eta$, $y=\eta+s$, $x=-\eta s$, so $x=\eta^2-\eta y$. Therefore the two branches are
$$
\boxed{u_\pm(x,y)=-\frac{y\pm\sqrt{y^2+4x}}2.}
$$
Each is $C^1$ on $D=\{y^2+4x>0\}$. The plus branch matches $u(0,y)=-y$ for $y>0$, whereas the minus branch matches it for $y<0$. Differentiating $u^2+yu-x=0$ gives $u_x=1/(2u+y)$ and $u_y=-u/(2u+y)$, verifying $u_y+uu_x=0$ wherever $2u+y\ne0$.

On the initial line, the normal <Characteristic curve> speed is $u=-y$, nonzero away from the origin. Thus the local existence theorem gives precisely these separate local branches near positive and negative initial points. At $y^2+4x=0$ the branches merge and their derivatives become singular. There is no $C^1$ solution realizing the entire initial line in a neighborhood of the origin: the data force $u_y(0,0)=-1$, while the equation and $u(0,0)=0$ force $u_y(0,0)=0$. The failure at this <Characteristic curve> point is a genuine limitation of the printed global initial-data request.

= 30A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Asymptotic Methods}

= Solution
{parent=30A}

In $I(\lambda)$ put $t=u/\sqrt\lambda$. Expanding the cubic exponential near the endpoint $t=0$ gives
$$
I(\lambda)\sim\lambda^{-\alpha-1/2}\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^n}{n!\lambda^{n/2}}\int_0^\infty u^{2\alpha+3n}e^{-u^2}\,du.
$$
The substitution $v=u^2$ evaluates the coefficient using the <Gamma function>. Thus, numbering the first term by $n=0$,
$$
\boxed{I(\lambda)\sim\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^n\Gamma(\alpha+(3n+1)/2)}{2n!\,\lambda^{\alpha+(n+1)/2}}.}
$$
For any fixed truncation the exponential's Taylor remainder is bounded by the next power times $e^{-u^2}$, and replacing the finite upper limit by infinity adds an exponentially small tail. This justifies the <asymptotic expansion> for $\alpha>-1/2$.

For large $n$, the ratio of magnitudes of consecutive terms, by <Stirling's formula>, is
$$
\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+(3n+4)/2)}{(n+1)\sqrt\lambda\,\Gamma(\alpha+(3n+1)/2)}
\sim\left(\frac32\right)^{3/2}\sqrt{\frac n\lambda}.
$$
The least term consequently has index $\boxed{n\sim8\lambda/27}$. An indexing convention starting at one changes this by one, without changing the estimate.

For the second integral, both endpoints minimize $t^2-t^3$. The endpoint $t=0$ gives the same expansion with positive coefficients. Its first two contributions are
$$
\boxed{J(\lambda)=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1/2)}{2\lambda^{\alpha+1/2}}+
\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+2)}{2\lambda^{\alpha+1}}+O(\lambda^{-1}),\quad-1/2<\alpha<0.}
$$
To check their ordering, near $t=1$ write $t=1-v/\lambda$. Then $\lambda(t^2-t^3)=v+O(v^2/\lambda)$ and $t^{2\alpha}=1+O(v/\lambda)$, producing a contribution $1/\lambda+O(\lambda^{-2})$. Because $\alpha+1<1$, this is smaller than the second displayed term. It is the third term, ahead of the next $t=0$ contribution of order $\lambda^{-\alpha-3/2}$. Splitting the integral into small endpoint neighborhoods and an interior interval, where the phase is strictly positive, makes the endpoint estimates rigorous.

= 31C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Integrable Systems}

= Solution
{parent=31C}

A <Hamiltonian system> with $n$ <degrees of freedom> is <Liouville integrable> if it has $n$ functionally independent <first integrals>, including the <Hamiltonian>, whose pairwise <Poisson brackets> vanish. <Functional independence> is required on a regular open set. The <Arnold-Liouville theorem> says that a connected compact regular common level of these integrals is an $n$-torus, with a neighborhood admitting <action-angle coordinates>; the <Hamiltonian> flow there has constant angular velocities and fixed actions. Compactness is essential to the torus conclusion.

Use $\{f,g\}=\sum_j(f_{q_j}g_{p_j}-f_{p_j}g_{q_j})$. On $r>0$, for differentiable $F$, <Hamilton's equations> give $\dot q=p$, $\dot p=-F'(r)q/r$. Hence the <angular momentum> $M=q\times p$ obeys
$$
\dot M=\dot q\times p+q\times\dot p=p\times p-\frac{F'(r)}r q\times q=0.
$$
In particular $M_1$ and $M_2$ are <first integrals>. The <Jacobi identity for the Poisson bracket> is
$$
\{f,\{g,h\}\}+\{g,\{h,f\}\}+\{h,\{f,g\}\}=0.
$$
Taking $f=H,g=M_1,h=M_2$ shows $\{H,\{M_1,M_2\}\}=0$. Direct differentiation gives $M_3=\{M_1,M_2\}=q_1p_2-q_2p_1$ and, cyclically, $\{M_i,M_j\}=\epsilon_{ijk}M_k$.

Set $L^2=M_1^2+M_2^2+M_3^2$. Then $\{L^2,M_3\}=2M_1(-M_2)+2M_2M_1=0$, and the brackets of $H$ with both expressions vanish. Thus
$$
\boxed{H,\ L^2,\ M_3\text{ are three commuting first integrals}.}
$$
For <functional independence>, at $q=(r,0,0)$ with $r>0$ the <Jacobian matrix> of $(H,L^2,M_3)$ with respect to $(p_1,p_2,p_3)$ is
$$
\begin{pmatrix}p_1&p_2&p_3\\0&2r^2p_2&2r^2p_3\\0&r&0\end{pmatrix},
$$
whose <determinant> is $-2r^3p_1p_3$. It is nonzero when $p_1p_3\ne0$. More generally this minor is a nonzero polynomial in $q,p$, independent of $F$, so its zero set has empty interior and the integrals are independent on a dense open regular set. This proves <Liouville integrability> on the regular set. The arbitrary potential in the question does not ensure compact common levels: $F=0$ has unbounded free trajectories. The full torus conclusion applies to those regular connected common levels which are compact, rather than to every choice of $F$ and every level.

= 32D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= a
{parent=32d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $D=[A,B]$, so $[A,D]=[B,D]=0$. Repeated use of the product rule for the <commutator> gives $[A,B^n]=nDB^{n-1}$. Summing the exponential series yields
$$
\boxed{[A,e^B]=De^B.}
$$
These manipulations hold for <bounded operators>, or as identities on a common invariant domain where the series and derivatives are justified.

The same commutation relation implies $e^{\lambda A}Be^{-\lambda A}=B+\lambda D$: its derivative is $e^{\lambda A}[A,B]e^{-\lambda A}=D$ and its initial value is $B$. Put $G(\lambda)=e^{\lambda A}e^{\lambda B}$. Then
$$
G'=(A+B+\lambda D)G.
$$
For the specified $F(\lambda)=G(\lambda)e^{-\lambda(A+B)}$, the operator $G$ commutes with $A+B$. Indeed conjugation by $e^{\lambda B}$ sends $A+B$ to $A+B-\lambda D$, and subsequent conjugation by $e^{\lambda A}$ restores $A+B$. Therefore
$$
F'=(A+B+\lambda D)F-F(A+B)=\lambda DF,\qquad F(0)=I,
$$
so $F(\lambda)=e^{\lambda^2D/2}$. At $\lambda=1$ this proves the central-commutator case of the <Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff formula>:
$$
\boxed{e^Ae^B=e^{[A,B]/2}e^{A+B}=e^{A+B}e^{[A,B]/2}.}
$$
The last equality holds because $D$ commutes with $A+B$.

= b
{parent=32d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <spatial translation operator> is $U(\alpha)=e^{-i\alpha\widehat p/\hbar}$. From $[\widehat x,\widehat p]=i\hbar$ and the preceding central-commutator result,
$$
\boxed{[\widehat x,U(\alpha)]=\alpha U(\alpha).}
$$
Thus $\widehat xU|x\rangle=U\widehat x|x\rangle+\alpha U|x\rangle=(x+\alpha)U|x\rangle$, consistent with $U|x\rangle=|x+\alpha\rangle$. Since $\langle x|U=\langle x-\alpha|$, the translated <wavefunction> is $\boxed{\psi(x-\alpha)}$.

For the <quantum harmonic oscillator>, the <creation and annihilation operators> give $\widehat p=i\sqrt{m\hbar\omega/2}(a^\dagger-a)$. Put $s=\alpha\sqrt{m\omega/(2\hbar)}$. The central <commutator> of $sa^\dagger$ and $-sa$ is $s^2$, so
$$
U=e^{s(a^\dagger-a)}=e^{-s^2/2}e^{sa^\dagger}e^{-sa}.
$$
As $a|0\rangle=0$ and $(a^\dagger)^n|0\rangle=\sqrt{n!}|n\rangle$,
$$
U|0\rangle=e^{-s^2/2}\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{s^n}{\sqrt{n!}}|n\rangle.
$$
Taking position <wavefunctions> proves
$$
\boxed{\psi_0(x-\alpha)=e^{-m\omega\alpha^2/(4\hbar)}\sum_{n=0}^\infty\left(\frac{m\omega}{2\hbar}\right)^{n/2}\frac{\alpha^n}{\sqrt{n!}}\psi_n(x).}
$$
The coefficient squares sum to $e^{-s^2}\sum_n s^{2n}/n!=1$, checking normalization of this <coherent state>.

= 33E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=33E}

For a spherically symmetric potential, take the polar axis along the momentum transfer $q$. Its angular integral is
$$
\int e^{-iqr\cos\vartheta}\,d\Omega=2\pi\int_{-1}^1e^{-iqr v}\,dv=4\pi\frac{\sin(qr)}{qr}.
$$
The <first Born approximation> consequently gives $f(\theta)=-2m(\hbar^2q)^{-1}\int_0^\infty rV(r)\sin(qr)\,dr$. The incident and scattered <wave vectors> both have magnitude $k$, so $q^2=2k^2(1-\cos\theta)$ and $\boxed{q=2k\sin(\theta/2)}$.

For the exponential potential, $\int_0^\infty re^{-(1-iq)r}\,dr=(1-iq)^{-2}$. Taking the imaginary part gives $2q/(1+q^2)^2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f(\theta)=-\frac{4mV_0}{\hbar^2[1+4k^2\sin^2(\theta/2)]^2}.}
$$
The <differential scattering cross section> is proportional to $[1+q^2]^{-4}$. At large $k$, substantial intensity requires $q=O(1)$, hence $\theta=O(k^{-1})$. For a precise convention, the half-intensity cone has half-angle $\theta_{1/2}\sim\sqrt{2^{1/4}-1}/k$. Its full angular width is twice that. The exponential's range has been set to one; restoring range $a$ gives width of order $1/(ka)$. As <energy> increases, the scattering therefore concentrates into the forward cone.

= 34D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Electrodynamics}

= Solution
{parent=34D}

Combining the real transformations gives
$$
F'_x=F_x,\qquad F'_y=\gamma(F_y+ivF_z),\qquad F'_z=\gamma(F_z-ivF_y).
$$
Let $v=\tanh\psi$, so $\gamma=\cosh\psi$ and $\gamma v=\sinh\psi$. The usual rotation about the positive $x$-axis has the $yz$ block $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\\sin\theta&\cos\theta\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Taking $\boxed{n=(1,0,0),\ \theta=-i\psi}$ gives exactly the displayed transformation, because $\cos(-i\psi)=\cosh\psi$ and $\sin(-i\psi)=-i\sinh\psi$. This is a complex rotation, not a real spatial rotation.

Complex rotations preserve the bilinear dot product, without conjugation. Hence
$$
F\cdot F=E^2-B^2+2iE\cdot B
$$
is invariant, giving the two independent real <Lorentz invariants>
$$
\boxed{E^2-B^2,\qquad E\cdot B.}
$$
Their completeness refers to scalar invariants of the full <Lorentz group>. For a generic non-null field, a boost along $E\times B$ makes the fields parallel. Indeed with $s=|E\times B|$ and $A=E^2+B^2$, the required speed solves $s(1+v^2)-Av=0$; the smaller root is $v=[A-\sqrt{(E^2-B^2)^2+4(E\cdot B)^2}]/(2s)<1$. For $s=0$ the fields are already parallel. A real rotation then gives $E=e n$, $B=b n$, leaving just $e^2-b^2$ and $eb$ as independent scalar parameters. Thus there are precisely two independent continuous scalar invariants on generic orbits; null fields have special orbits and the invariants do not distinguish zero from a nonzero null field.

If the printed phrase is read as invariance under only this single-axis boost, it admits extra fixed components $F_x$, so the assertion of precisely two must be understood in its Lorentz-scalar sense.

= 35E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=General Relativity}

= Solution
{parent=35E}

The metric is nonsingular on $r>0$; $r=0$ is a coordinate boundary at which the displayed coefficients diverge. The nonzero <Christoffel symbols> are
$$
\Gamma^t_{tr}=\Gamma^t_{rt}=-1/r,\qquad \Gamma^r_{tt}=-1/r,\qquad \Gamma^r_{rr}=-1/r.
$$
Writing dots for derivatives with respect to <proper time>, the <geodesic equations> are
$$
\boxed{\ddot t-2\dot t\dot r/r=0,\qquad \ddot r-(\dot t^2+\dot r^2)/r=0.}
$$
The first gives $\dot t=kr^2$ for a constant $k$, chosen positive for a future-directed particle. Normalization of a timelike four-velocity gives $(-\dot t^2+\dot r^2)/r^2=-1$, so $\dot r^2=k^2r^4-r^2$. Dividing by $\dot t^2$ gives
$$
\boxed{(dr/dt)^2=1-1/(k^2r^2).}
$$
Integrating on each side of the turning point yields $\sqrt{r^2-k^{-2}}=|t-t_0|$, hence $r^2-(t-t_0)^2=k^{-2}$. Choose $t_0=0$ and let $\tau=0$ at the minimum radius. Then
$$
\boxed{kr=\sec\tau,\qquad kt=\tan\tau,\qquad -\pi/2<\tau<\pi/2.}
$$
Differentiating verifies $\dot t=\sec^2\tau/k=kr^2$, the normalization and the radial <Geodesic equation>. <proper time> therefore remains finite as the coordinate radius and time tend to infinity at the endpoints of this chart.

= 36A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics II}

= Solution
{parent=36A}

Let $e_{ij}=(\partial_i u_j+\partial_j u_i)/2$ be the <rate-of-strain tensor>. A <Newtonian fluid> has a linear isotropic constitutive law for its viscous <stress tensor>. To determine its form, diagonalize the symmetric tensor $e$. Rotations by $\pi$ about its principal axes leave $e$ fixed, forcing its stress response to be diagonal as well. Linearity and symmetry under permutations of the axes force each diagonal entry to be $\lambda\operatorname{tr}e+2\mu e_{ii}$, with the same constants for each axis. Rotating back gives $\tau_{ij}=\lambda e_{kk}\delta_{ij}+2\mu e_{ij}$. Since <incompressibility> means $e_{kk}=0$, adding the arbitrary isotropic pressure gives
$$
\boxed{\sigma_{ij}=-p\delta_{ij}+2\mu e_{ij}.}
$$
At a material interface between ordinary viscous fluids without interfacial slip, velocity is continuous, and its normal component equals the interface's normal speed. Tangential traction is continuous when <surface tension> is constant. Normal traction has the curvature-dependent <surface tension> jump; in a convention defining signed curvature $\kappa$ by the balance, $(\sigma_+-\sigma_-)n=\gamma\kappa n$. With no <surface tension>, the full traction is continuous. At rigid stationary walls the <no-slip boundary condition> imposes zero velocity.

Put $G=-dp/dx>0$ and seek $u=u_\pm(y)e_x$. The convective acceleration vanishes, and the <Navier–Stokes equations> reduce to $\mu_\pm u_\pm''=-G$, with $p=p_0-Gx$. <continuity> of velocity and tangential traction at $y=0$ gives a common interface velocity $U_0$ and a common shear integration constant $C$:
$$
u_\pm(y)=U_0+\frac C{\mu_\pm}y-\frac G{2\mu_\pm}y^2.
$$
Imposing $u_-(-a)=u_+(a)=0$ gives
$$
\boxed{U_0=\frac{Ga^2}{\mu_++\mu_-},\qquad C=\frac{Ga(\mu_--\mu_+)}{2(\mu_++\mu_-)}.}
$$
The velocity has no normal component, so the flat interface remains stationary, and its normal stresses match because pressure is common. These expressions give the <two-layer plane Poiseuille flow with unequal viscosities>.

= a
{parent=36a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The signed tangential stress component is $\sigma_{xy}=\mu u'=C-Gy$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\sigma_{xy}(-a)=\frac{Ga(\mu_++3\mu_-)}{2(\mu_++\mu_-)},\qquad
\sigma_{xy}(a)=-\frac{Ga(3\mu_++\mu_-)}{2(\mu_++\mu_-)}.}
$$
When $\mu_+/\mu_-\to1$, these approach $+Ga$ and $-Ga$, the usual symmetric <plane Poiseuille flow> stresses. When $\mu_+/\mu_-\to\infty$, they approach $+Ga/2$ and $-3Ga/2$. The upper fluid's velocity tends to zero, but its viscosity times velocity gradient has a finite limit. These are tensor components; traction on a chosen wall normal includes that normal's sign.

= b
{parent=36a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Integrating the velocity profiles over their respective half-channels gives <volume flux> per unit width
$$
Q_-=\frac{Ga^3(\mu_++7\mu_-)}{12\mu_-(\mu_++\mu_-)},\qquad
Q_+=\frac{Ga^3(7\mu_++\mu_-)}{12\mu_+(\mu_++\mu_-)}.
$$
With $r=\mu_+/\mu_-$, the requested ratio is
$$
\boxed{Q_+/Q_-=\frac{1+7r}{r(7+r)}.}
$$
At $r=1$, both fluxes equal $Ga^3/(3\mu_-)$ and the ratio is one. For $r\to\infty$, $Q_-\to Ga^3/(12\mu_-)$, $Q_+\sim7Ga^3/(12\mu_+)$ and the ratio is asymptotic to $7/r$, tending to zero. The lower fluid flows between the wall and an interface which becomes effectively stationary; the upper fluid carries vanishing flux despite finite limiting stress.

= 37B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Waves}

= Solution
{parent=37B}

The linear acoustic momentum and <continuity> equations are $\rho u_t=-p_x$ and $p_t=-\rho c^2u_x$. For a right-moving <plane wave> depending on $x-ct$, integration gives $\boxed{p=\rho c\,u}$, with zero perturbation constants. For a left-moving wave the sign is reversed. The factor $Z=\rho c$ is the <acoustic impedance>. Proportionality applies to a traveling wave; a superposition of opposite directions generally has no single such constant.

Use time dependence $e^{-i\omega t}$ and set $Z_j=\rho_jc_j$. In the outer regions write pressures $I e^{ik_0x}+R e^{-ik_0x}$ and $T I e^{ik_0x}$, taking $I=1$ for convenience. The corresponding velocities are $(e^{ik_0x}-R e^{-ik_0x})/Z_0$ and $Te^{ik_0x}/Z_0$. Pressure and normal velocity are continuous at both layer boundaries. Within the cooled layer a decomposition into right and left waves gives, with $\delta=k_1L$,
$$
\begin{pmatrix}p(L)\\u(L)\end{pmatrix}=
\begin{pmatrix}\cos\delta&iZ_1\sin\delta\\ i\sin\delta/Z_1&\cos\delta\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}p(0)\\u(0)\end{pmatrix}.
$$
This matrix follows by writing $p=Ae^{ik_1x}+Be^{-ik_1x}$ and $Z_1u=Ae^{ik_1x}-Be^{-ik_1x}$. Applying its inverse and using $p(0)+Z_0u(0)=2$ eliminates $R$, giving
$$
T=\frac{e^{-ik_0L}}{\cos\delta-\frac i2(\lambda+\lambda^{-1})\sin\delta},\qquad \lambda=Z_1/Z_0.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{|T|=\left[\cos^2(k_1L)+\frac14(\lambda+\lambda^{-1})^2\sin^2(k_1L)\right]^{-1/2}.}
$$
For $\lambda\gg1$, transmission is weak except near $k_1L=n\pi$, where $|T|=1$. The cooled layer acts as a resonant filter: an <integer> number of half-wavelengths fits within it at each <resonance>. The half-power width in $\delta$ is asymptotically $2/\lambda$ on either side. Since the two outer impedances agree, the transmitted power fraction is $|T|^2$.

= 38C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= a
{parent=38c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Number the interior grid vertices in any order. The diagonal of $A$ is $10/3$, and off-diagonal weights are $-2/3$ for axial neighbors and $-1/6$ for diagonal neighbors. Each link weight is symmetric, so $A$ is symmetric. Extend the interior grid values by zero to the boundary. Since the eight stencil weights sum to $10/3$, grouping links gives
$$
u^TAu=\sum_{\{i,j\}\text{ interior link}}w_{ij}(u_i-u_j)^2+
\sum_{i\text{ interior},\ j\text{ boundary link}}w_{ij}u_i^2,
$$
where $w_{ij}=2/3$ or $1/6$ and each interior link is counted once. Every term is nonnegative. If the <quadratic form> vanishes, neighboring interior values agree, and every vertex linked to the boundary has value zero. The axial-link grid is connected and reaches the boundary, so all values are zero. Thus $\boxed{A\text{ is symmetric positive definite}}$. Reordering vertices replaces $A$ by $PAP^T$ for a <permutation matrix> $P$, preserving both properties.

= b
{parent=38c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a linear system write $A=D+R$, where $D$ is the diagonal. The <Jacobi method> iterates
$$
u^{(k+1)}=D^{-1}(b-Ru^{(k)}),\qquad e^{(k+1)}=Be^{(k)},\quad B=-D^{-1}R.
$$
Here $D=(10/3)I$, and $B$ has nonnegative off-diagonal entries with row sums at most one. Rows adjacent to the boundary have sum strictly less than one; those at purely interior stencil vertices have sum one. Thus $\|B\|_\infty\leq1$.

To prove strict <spectral radius> below one, suppose $Bv=\zeta v$ with $|\zeta|=1$, and choose an index with $|v_i|=\|v\|_\infty>0$. In
$$
|v_i|=|\zeta v_i|\leq\sum_jB_{ij}|v_j|\leq\left(\sum_jB_{ij}\right)\|v\|_\infty\leq\|v\|_\infty,
$$
equality forces that row sum to equal one and every linked neighbor to attain the same maximum modulus. Propagate this along the connected axial grid to a boundary-adjacent row, whose row sum is strictly less than one, a contradiction. All <eigenvalues> therefore have modulus strictly below one. Hence $B^k\to0$ and the Jacobi errors tend to zero from every initial vector. The unique solution exists by positive definiteness, so $\boxed{\text{Jacobi converges for this discretization in any ordering}.}$
